ESP32_FIRMWARE_SPEC (TEXT)
Project: RideOn ESP32 Firmware (MicroPython)
Document type: Requirements + Design Specification (Firmware)
Target MCU: ESP32-WROOM (MicroPython)
Date: 2026-04-11

PURPOSE & SCOPE
0.1 Purpose
The ESP32 firmware provides safe motor control of a ride-on car using PWM motor control, BLE remote control, and safety monitoring (temperature, current, battery voltage).

0.2 Scope
The firmware shall:

Provide BLE peripheral services for receiving commands and sending telemetry.
Drive throttle and steering using PWM + direction GPIO.
Monitor battery voltage, battery current, and five temperature sensors.
Apply safety shutdown conditions with latched faults.
Prevent motor activation until boot-time current sensor calibration completes.

Out of scope (for now):

Runtime fault recovery (no automatic clearing).
Sophisticated filtering of current/temperature beyond boot-time calibration stability.
Advanced diagnostics beyond fault flags and scalar telemetry.


SYSTEM OVERVIEW
1.1 Control roles


ESP32 acts as BLE Peripheral.
Smartphone app acts as BLE Central.

1.2 Control architecture (layers)
A layered design is used for readability and safety:
Layer A: Hardware Abstraction

PWM outputs
Direction GPIO outputs
ADC reads
I2C sensor reads

Layer B: Safety Manager

Computes max temperature
Computes battery voltage
Computes battery current (calibrated)
Latches faults and stops motors

Layer C: Control Translation

Maps BLE command (int8 throttle, int8 steer) to PWM duty and direction bits

Layer D: BLE Communications

RX characteristic receives 2-byte commands
TX characteristic notifies telemetry packet


MOTOR CONTROL (PWM + DIRECTION BIT)
2.1 Outputs
There are no forward/reverse relay pins.

Each axis uses:

1 PWM output (magnitude)
1 direction GPIO output (sign)

Throttle:

THR_PWM: PWM magnitude
THR_DIR: HIGH = forward, LOW = reverse

Steering:

STR_PWM: PWM magnitude
STR_DIR: HIGH = right, LOW = left

2.2 PWM parameters

PWM frequency: 20 kHz recommended (configurable)
PWM duty range: 0..PWM_MAX (e.g. 0..1023)

2.3 Command mapping
Incoming command values are signed int8:

throttle: -127..+127
steering: -127..+127

For each axis:

If value > 0: direction pin = HIGH, duty proportional to value
If value < 0: direction pin = LOW, duty proportional to abs(value)
If value == 0: duty = 0 (motor off)

2.4 Motor enable gating
Motor outputs shall be gated by a boolean motors_enabled.
If motors_enabled == False:

PWM duty MUST be forced to 0 regardless of BLE commands.


BLE PROFILE (GATT)
3.1 Device name


The BLE advertising name shall be “RideOn”.

3.2 GATT UUIDs
Service UUID:

6E400001-B5A3-F393-E0A9-E50E24DCCA9E

RX Characteristic (Write):

6E400002-B5A3-F393-E0A9-E50E24DCCA9E
Properties: WRITE and/or WRITE_NO_RESPONSE

TX Characteristic (Notify):

6E400003-B5A3-F393-E0A9-E50E24DCCA9E
Properties: NOTIFY

3.3 Downlink command payload (Android -> ESP32)
Length: exactly 2 bytes:

Byte 0: throttle int8
Byte 1: steering int8

3.4 Uplink telemetry payload (ESP32 -> Android)
Telemetry notification packet is 7 bytes encoded big-endian as:

hHHB

Fields:

int16 temp_cC  : max temperature among 5 sensors in °C * 100
uint16 vbat_cV : battery voltage in V * 100
uint16 curr_cA : battery current in A * 100 (absolute value)
uint8 flags    : fault bitmask


TEMPERATURE MEASUREMENT
4.1 Sensors


5 temperature sensors are AHT10 (I2C, fixed address).
No I2C multiplexer is used.
Each AHT10 sensor is wired to its own dedicated I2C bus (separate SDA/SCL pin pairs).
The firmware does not need to identify sensors individually; only max temperature is used.

4.2 Max temperature

The firmware shall compute the maximum of all 5 sensor temperatures.
This maximum temperature is used for:

Overtemperature shutdown
Telemetry reporting (only max is sent)



4.3 Overtemperature threshold

Threshold: 70.0 °C
Condition: if max_temp >= 70.0 °C -> overtemperature fault latched

4.4 Sensor read failures
If a temperature read fails:

Firmware should treat it as unsafe and latch an overtemperature fault (or set a dedicated sensor-failure fault in a future extension).
Motors must stop.


BATTERY VOLTAGE MEASUREMENT
5.1 Divider ratio


Voltage divider ratio is 1:25 (Vbat = Vadc * 25).

5.2 ADC range

ESP32 ADC range is 0..3.3 V (with appropriate attenuation).
The divider allows measuring up to approx 82 V; expected battery <= 18 V.

5.3 Undervoltage and overvoltage thresholds

Undervoltage: Vbat <= 10.0 V -> undervoltage fault latched
Overvoltage: Vbat >= 20.0 V -> overvoltage fault latched


CURRENT MEASUREMENT (ACS754-050B + ADC)
6.1 Sensor type


ACS754LCB-050B (50 A range)
Output approximately 0.5..4.5 V around 2.5 V at 0 A
Sensitivity approx 40 mV/A (nominal)

6.2 ADC interface

Because output is up to 5 V, the sensor output shall be halved by a 1:1 divider before ESP32 ADC input.
Therefore:

ADC-side nominal zero voltage is approx 1.25 V.
Sensor-side nominal zero voltage is approx 2.5 V.



6.3 Calibration model
A first-order calibration model is used:

Offset (ACS_ZERO_V) is calibrated at boot.
Gain uses a fixed constant (ACS_GAIN_V_PER_A), hardcoded for now.

6.4 Boot-time calibration requirement

Firmware shall perform current sensor offset calibration at boot.
Motors must be disabled during calibration.
Calibration uses:

settle delay (typ. 200 ms)
N samples (typ. 41)
median estimate for zero offset (reject spikes)


Calibration result is stored as ACS_ZERO_V (sensor-side volts).

6.5 Calibration sanity check requirement
Calibration must pass a sanity window:

Expected ADC-side zero voltage: 1.25 V
Allowed tolerance: ±10% of 1.25 V => ±0.125 V
Therefore valid window:

1.125 V <= adc_zero <= 1.375 V
If the measured median adc_zero is outside this window:


Latch calibration invalid fault.
Keep motors disabled.
Report fault via telemetry.
Only power cycle clears it.

6.6 Overcurrent threshold

Overcurrent: abs(current) >= 40.0 A -> overcurrent fault latched
Rationale: expected typical max current ~25 A; 40 A is permissive; any sample above is abnormal.

6.7 No runtime filtering requirement

Runtime current filtering (moving average) is not required and should not add latency.
Overcurrent shall trigger on a single sample above threshold.
(Future option: minimal 2-sample confirm if false trips occur, but not required now.)


FAULT HANDLING POLICY (LATCHED, POWER-CYCLE RESET ONLY)
7.1 Fault policy
If any fault occurs (overtemp/overcurrent/undervolt/overvolt/calibration invalid):


Motors must stop immediately (PWM duty = 0).
Fault must latch (persist in RAM state).
Motors remain disabled until power cycle.
No runtime recovery mechanism exists.
BLE telemetry shall continue (if possible) to report the fault reason.

7.2 Fault flags bitmask
Fault flags are latched in a uint8:

bit0: overtemperature
bit1: overcurrent
bit2: undervoltage
bit3: overvoltage
bit4: calibration invalid
bit5..7: reserved

7.3 Reset policy

The only way to clear faults is a full power cycle (POWERON reset).
BLE reconnect, commands, or UI actions do not clear faults.


BOOT SEQUENCE REQUIREMENTS
8.1 Safe defaults
On boot:


motors_enabled = False
PWM duty = 0 (throttle and steering)
Direction pins set to a safe state

8.2 Calibration gate

Perform boot-time current sensor calibration.
If calibration valid: calibration_done = True
motors_enabled shall be set True only if:

calibration_done is True
fault_latched is False



8.3 Motors locked until calibration complete

Firmware must not apply BLE drive commands before calibration completion.


MAIN LOOP & TIMING
9.1 Safety check frequency


Safety checks should run periodically (e.g. 20 ms loop).
Telemetry can be sent at a slower rate (e.g. 500 ms).

9.2 BLE RX processing

BLE RX write handler parses the two command bytes and applies drive output only if motors_enabled and no fault_latched.

9.3 Telemetry rate

Telemetry notifications recommended: 2 Hz (every 500 ms).


ACCEPTANCE TESTS (FIRMWARE)
10.1 Boot calibration


On boot, verify motors remain off until calibration completes.
Verify calibration invalid window triggers fault latch if sensor wiring is wrong.

10.2 Command control

When connected, sending throttle/steering should drive correct PWM and direction pins.
When disconnected, motor outputs should stop.

10.3 Fault latching
Induce each fault condition and verify:

Motors stop immediately
Fault flag sets correctly
Fault remains latched until power cycle

10.4 Telemetry

Verify telemetry packet fields decode correctly on Android:

temp, vbat, current, flags



END OF DOCUMENT